# Cline Rules for local-ai-platform

## Core Protocol

- Read these rules before making changes.
- Treat the latest user-provided known state as authoritative over older context.
- Keep changes scoped to the requested files and task.
- At the top of every report, state changed files.
- If a task says planning-only, do not edit files.
- If a task says docs-only, source/test/env/log/memory files are read-only unless explicitly allowed.

## Windows Commands

- Use PowerShell on Windows.
- Prefer short, separate commands over dense chained one-liners.
- Do not use very long PowerShell replacement commands; command transport may wrap them and inject newlines.
- If a command fails twice, stop and report:
  - exact command
  - exit code
  - stdout
  - stderr
  - known state after failure
- Do not silently invent fallback strategies after repeated failure.

## File Edits

- Prefer the normal edit tool for small edits.
- If an edit tool returns `undefined`, `EISDIR`, "SEARCH does not match" despite freshly-read matching content, or "User closed text editor", treat it as tool failure.
- Retry once with a smaller exact block.
- If it fails again, stop and ask for guidance.
- Avoid broad scripted rewrites.
- Preserve existing line endings. Follow `.editorconfig`.
- Use ASCII unless the file already contains or requires Unicode.
- After edits, run:
  - `git diff --check`
  - `git diff -- <changed-files>`

## Tool Transport Failures

- Do not narrate or emit internal tool-call XML/JSON syntax in user-facing reports.
- If a tool reports a missing required parameter, empty command, empty path, or malformed invocation, treat it as a tool transport/formatting failure, not a repository failure.
- Retry once with the smallest valid invocation.
- For `attempt_completion` and final reports, keep retries compact. Long final reports may be dropped or malformed by transport.
- If the same tool transport failure happens twice, stop and report:
  - intended action
  - exact tool error
  - whether files were modified
  - `git status --short`
- Avoid broad searches that can flood context. Scope grep/search to the requested files first; broaden only if needed.

## Context Economy

Every request resends the whole conversation, so anything read stays in context and is paid for again on every later turn.

- Read a file once. Read it again only if it changed since your last read: you edited it, a command you ran rewrote it, or `git status --short` / `git diff --stat` shows it changed.
- After your own edit, trust the edit tool's result and the diff instead of re-reading the whole file. Re-read only the edited region when you need to check it.
- When you need several files or several ranges, request them in one batched call, not one call per file.
- Read ranges, not whole files, when the task only needs part of a large file. Search first (scoped to the relevant paths) to find the range.
- Keep command output small: filter, `Select-Object -First N`, or count instead of printing large outputs. Never print a large file or log just to look at it.
- Do not repeat a command whose result is already in the conversation unless something it depends on changed.

## Gateway / Backend

- Gateway runs locally on port `8001` with `apps.gateway.main:create_app`.
- The live backend is vLLM at `http://100.106.236.88:18300/v1` (model `qwen3.8-flash-next`, client alias `qwen38-27b`). See `CLAUDE.md`, "Model routing".
- Port `30000` belongs to another workload. Never stop or modify anything on port `30000`.
- If source code or `.env` changed, restart the gateway before live probes.
- Before stopping gateway processes, list candidates and match command lines.
- Stop only processes whose command line contains both `uvicorn` and `apps.gateway.main:create_app`.
- If no gateway process is found and health fails, do not investigate further; start the gateway.

## Live Quality Probes

- Run only the requested probes.
- Write JSON outputs to requested paths.
- Do not persist to engineering memory unless explicitly instructed and clean-pass criteria are met.
- If gateway was not restarted after source/env changes, report that before running probes.

## Reporting

- Report exact commands run and exact verification output.
- Separate current-task changes from pre-existing uncommitted changes.
- Do not claim a clean state unless `git status --short` supports it.
